00:00:01.640 --> 00:00:03.889 align:start position:0%
in this episode of cloud performance  atlas we help some DNA technicians  improve their upload speeds while they  get the folds out of their genome before  their big meeting stay tuned to find out  DNA soap is a biotech company whose main  product is a software that does protein  folding across distributed machines  sadly though anytime they would provide  a new data set to be worked on there was  a significant delay before any clients  would start doing computation their  architecture was pretty straightforward  once a new genome data set came in it  would get chopped up and uploaded to  Google Cloud storage where clients who  were listening for pub/sub updates would  grab the work blocks and start computing  now here's the interesting part the size  of the work blocks change depending on  how many connected clients there were to  do the work so more workers resulted in  smaller work blocks to balance out  properly the problem at hand was that  the DNA soaps software was taking a  really long time to upload all the files  to GCS in regions with a really high  number of available workers to try and  replicate this I created a small Python  script which generated files of various  sizes and then uploaded each file to a  GCS regional bucket about a hundred  times or so turns out the smaller the  files in your set this slower your  upload speed the reason for this is that  there's a certain amount of  transactional overhead for uploading  files so the smaller they are the more  this overhead adds up the solution  parallel uploads gsutil provides the - M  option which performs parallel copy  which can significantly increase the  performance of an upload when we chart  the performance against our base state  we can see that the parallel version  provides significantly higher throughput  since the transactional overhead is  mitigated across many threads for DNA  soap this small command line flag  resulted in a 2x improvement in upload  speed for their files not bad for a  bunch of scientists if you want to know  more about parallel uploads check out  the associated article listed in the  description below and don't forget to  check out the rest of our cloud  performance Atlas content when it comes  to performance every millisecond counts
 

